''Melicytus ramiflorus'' is a shrub or small tree in the family [[Violaceae]]. It reaches {{convert|15|m|abbr=off}} in height, with a [[Trunk (botany)|trunk]] reaching {{convert|0.6–0.8|m|abbr=on}} in diameter, typically branched from near the base. The [[Bark (botany)|bark]] is greyish-white in colour, while the underside is bright green. The wood is soft, pale-brown or white-coloured, and brittle.{{Sfn|De Lange|2013}}{{Sfn|Kirk|1889|p=30}}

Leaves are thick and fleshy. They are {{convert|50–150 × 30–50|mm|abbr=on}} long, light to dark green in colour, narrow to oval-shaped; tips are pointed to long-pointed, or rarely blunt in character. The [[Leaf margin|margins]] are coarsely serrated, rarely nearly smooth or irregularly toothed.{{Sfn|De Lange|2013}} They are arranged in an alternating pattern.{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}} A distinct feature of ''M. ramiflorus'' are the skeletonised leaves on the [[leaf litter]], where the leaves retain their veins even after the rest of the tissue has decayed.{{Sfn|Vennell|2014}}{{Sfn|Dawson|Lucas|2011|p=260}}

''Melicytus ramiflorus'' is characterised by its flowers and purple fruits, which grow directly from the woody stems, a trait known as [[ramiflory]].{{Sfn|Vennell|2014}} Flowering occurs from November to February. The [[inflorescence]]s (flower clusters) grow either from the stems directly or at the end of them. Each flower is 3–4&nbsp;mm in diameter.{{Sfn|De Lange|2013}} They are [[dioecious]], meaning male and female flowers are on separate plants.{{Sfn|Burrows|1995|p=3}} The [[Pedicel (botany)|pedicels]] are 5–10&nbsp;mm long. The [[petal]]s are typically greenish-yellow in colour. Fruits are found in [[berries]] which are violet, dark blue or purple in colour, 4–5&nbsp;mm in diameter, ovoid-like to globe-shaped in character.{{Sfn|De Lange|2013}} Fruiting occurs from November to March. There are three–six, potentially ten, seeds per berry.{{Sfn|De Lange|2013}}{{Sfn|Burrows|1995|p=3}} ''M. ramiflorus'' has a [[diploid]] chromosome count of 32.{{Sfn|De Lange|2013}}

==Taxonomy==
The plant was first [[Species description|described]] by the German naturalists [[Georg Forster|Georg]] and [[Johann Reinhold Forster]] in 1776.{{Sfn|POWO (1)|2026}} There are nineteen species of the ''[[Melicytus]]'' genus currently accepted by the [[Plants of the World Online]] taxonomic database. These species are native to Australia and several islands in the Pacific.{{Sfn|POWO (2)|2026}} ''M. ramiflorus'' is the [[type species]] of the genus ''Melicytus''.{{sfn|Heenan|Courtney|De Lange|Molloy|2018|p=11}} Mitchell et al. (2009) suggested that ''Melicytus'' first [[Divergent evolution|diverged]] in New Zealand, and then diverged elsewhere.{{Sfn|Mitchell|Heenan|Murray|Molloy|2009|p=1}}

Historic classifications identified four [[subspecies]] within ''M. ramiflorus'', including subsp. ''oblongifolius'' from [[Norfolk Island]], subsp. ''fastigiata'' from [[Fiji]], and subsp. ''samoensis'' from [[Samoa]]. Art Whistler argues that each of these should be treated as separate species rather than subspecies. The [[New Zealand Plant Conservation Network]] followed that opinion.{{Sfn|De Lange|2013}}

The Plants of the World Online taxonomic database recognises two subspecies of ''Melicytus ramiflorus'':{{Sfn|POWO (1)|2026}}
*''Melicytus ramiflorus'' subsp. ''ramiflorus'' — native to mainland New Zealand and the Kermadec Islands
*''Melicytus ramiflorus'' subsp. ''oblongifolius'' — native to the Norfolk Islands

===Etymology===
The [[etymology]] (word origin) of the [[genus]] name, ''Melicytus'', is uncertain, but is possibly derived from the [[Greek language|Greek]] words {{lang|el|μєλι}} (meli), meaning 'honey', and {{lang|el|κuτος}} (kytos), meaning 'hollow container', literally meaning 'honey-cave'.{{Sfn|De Lange|2013}}{{Sfn|Gledhill|2008|p=255}} The [[specific epithet]] (second part of the scientific name), ''ramiflorus'', means 'branch-flowering', from the [[Latin]] {{lang|la|ramus}}, meaning 'branch', and {{lang|la|flos}} meaning 'flower'. The species is [[Common name|commonly known]] as māhoe, hinahina, and whiteywood.{{Sfn|De Lange|2013}} In older sources, the plant is sometimes called cowleaf or cowtree, referring to the plant being used as [[fodder]] given to livestock.{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}}{{Sfn|Aston|1918|p=324}}

