''Melicytus ramiflorus''{{'}}s seeds are [[Biological dispersal|dispersed]] by [[Frugivore|fruit-eating animals]] (frugivores), such as birds and lizards.{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}} ''M. ramiflorus'' is [[Pollination|pollinated]] by insects, although the nectar may be collected by bees, birds, [[flies]], and [[thrip]]s.{{Sfn|Powlesland|1984|p=1}}{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}} Burrows (1995) recorded ''M. ramiflorus''{{'}}s fruits are eaten by [[kererū]] (''Hemiphaga novaeseelandiae''), [[New Zealand bellbird]]s (''Anthornis melanura''), [[common blackbird]]s (''Turdus merula''), and [[silvereye]]s (''Zosterops lateralis'').{{Sfn|Burrows|1995|p=3}}{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}} Several other birds have been recorded eating the fruits, such as, [[tūī]] (''Prosthemadera novaeseelandiae''), [[kōkako]] (''Callaeas'' sp.), [[Whitehead (bird)|whitehead]]s (''Mohoua albicilla''), and [[kākāriki]] (''Cyanoramphus'' sp.).{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}} Deer, cattle, goats, horses, and possums eat the leaves.{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}}{{Sfn|Kirk|1889|p=30}}

Several beetles and insects feed on the plant, including the [[lemon tree borer]] (''Oemona hirta'') and the [[Epalxiphora axenana|sharp-tipped bell moth]] (''Epalxiphora axenana'').{{Sfn|Sopow|Bain|2017|pp=10–11}}{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}} The tunnelling by insect [[larva]]e can then be inhabited by [[wētā]].{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}} Powlesland (1984) studied the reproductive biology of three New Zealand ''Melicytus'' species and their floral visitors. ''M. ramiflorus''{{'}}s flowers were primarily visited by the [[Calliphora quadrimaculata| native bluebottle]] (''Calliphora quadrimaculata''), a fly species.{{Sfn|Powlesland|1984|p=7}} A species of ear fungus, ''[[Auricularia cornea]]'', is often found on the trunks.{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}} The lichen ''[[Pyrenula moniliformis]]'' is only known to grow on ''M. ramiflorus'' trees located in the [[Auckland Region]].{{sfn|Marshall|Blanchon|Aptroot|De Lange|2025|p=5}} A species of [[algal]] pathogen, ''[[Cephaleuros lagerheimii]]'', can be found on ''M. ramiflorus''.{{Sfn|Hood|2010|p=2}}

''Melicytus ramiflorus'' has low to moderate flammability rates, according to Wyse et al. (2016).{{sfn|Wyse|Perry|O'Connell|Holland|2016|pp=3, 7}} Dawson & Lucas (2011) states that, during flowering, ''M. ramiflorus'' can be [[semideciduous]], meaning they partially lose their leaves during a period of time. Leaf loss can also be caused by the [[Feredayia graminosa|māhoe moth]] (''Feredayia graminosa'').{{Sfn|Dawson|Lucas|2011|p=260}} ''M. ramiflorus'' has been suspected in causing deaths in cattle. Aston (1923) mentioned a [[veterinarian]] reported the branches and leaves may cause poisonings, but did not specify a toxin responsible for this. There have not been any other records of the plant being poisonous.{{Sfn|Aston|1918|p=324}}

==Distribution==
[[File:Melicytus ramiflorus - John Barkla - 5332743.jpeg|thumb|right|alt=Foliage of Melicytus ramiflorus|''Melicytus ramiflorus'' growing on the [[Banks Peninsula]]]]
''Melicytus ramiflorus'' subsp. ''ramiflorus'' is [[endemic]] to New Zealand, ''M. ramiflorus'' subsp. ''oblongifolius'' is endemic to the [[Norfolk Island]]s.{{Sfn|POWO (1)|2026}} ''M. ramiflorus'' is found throughout the [[North Island|North]] and [[South Island]]s, although its present on [[Stewart Island]] is rare.{{Sfn|Dawson|Lucas|2011|p=260}}{{Sfn|Kirk|1889|p=30}} The New Zealand botanist [[Donald Petrie (botanist)|Donald Petrie]] reported its presence there. The New Zealand botanist [[Thomas Kirk (botanist)|Thomas Kirk]] stated in his 1889 revision of the New Zealand flora that the species is "evidently rare in that locality".{{Sfn|Kirk|1889|p=30}} Dawson & Lucas (2011) mentions it is found there, however Crowe (2009) does not mention it.{{Sfn|Dawson|Lucas|2011|p=260}}{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}}  ''M. ramiflorus'' subsp. ''ramiflorus''{{'}}s 2023 assessment in the [[New Zealand Threat Classification System]] was "Not Threatened".{{Sfn|De Lange|2013}}

===Habitat===
''Melicytus ramiflorus'' commonly inhabits lowland to lower montane envrionments.{{Sfn|Burrows|1995|p=3}} It is often found in mixed angiosperm-podocarp forests, reaching {{convert|850-1000|m|abbr=on}} above sea level at maximum elevation.{{Sfn|Burrows|1995|p=3}}{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}} ''M. ramiflorus'' prefers moist, new surfaces, such as, glacial moraine and river banks.{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=94}} It can also be found on stony soils on terraces and landslides.{{Sfn|Dawson|Lucas|2011|p=260}} It is commonly associated with other light-demanding species, such as, [[Aristotelia serrata|wineberry]] (''Aristotelia serrata''), [[mānuka]] (''Leptospermum scoparium''), and [[Myrsine australis|red matipō]] (''Myrsine australis'').{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=94}}

==Uses==
There are several recorded [[Māori language]] names for the plant, including, {{lang|mi|hinahina}}, {{lang|mi|māhoe}}, and {{lang|mi|moeahu}}.{{Sfn|Manaaki Whenua|2020}} The primary use of ''M. ramiflorus'' for the indigenous [[Māori people]] was using the wood to generate fire by friction. The primary use of the plant for [[European settlers in New Zealand|European settlers]] was the burnt wood in the production of [[gunpowder]].{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=26}}{{Sfn|Kirk|1889|p=30}} In [[Rongoā|traditional Māori medicinal practices]], the inner bark was used to treat skin conditions and burns. The leaves were also of medicinal use.{{Sfn|Vennell|2014}}{{Sfn|Manaaki Whenua|2020}} Another use for Māori was the black juice from the berries being used for [[tā moko|tattoos]] ({{lang|mi|tā moko}}).{{Sfn|Manaaki Whenua|2020}}

